How to install Imperium:

Imperium builds on Linux. You need a C compiler, make, and the crypt
library headers (on Debian/Ubuntu: "apt install build-essential
libcrypt-dev"; on Fedora: "dnf install gcc make libxcrypt-devel").

1)	Run "make" in this directory (the top level of the Imperium source
	distribution). There is no configure step any more.

	The first build asks for three secret codes which are saved in
	"impsec.h". To build without being asked (scripts, Docker, etc.)
	set IMP_CON_CODE, IMP_CTRL_CODE and IMP_SHUT_CODE in the
	environment, or run make with no terminal attached and random codes
	will be generated. Keep impsec.h - every program must be built with
	the same codes, and the server must be restarted after the codes
	change. "make clean" keeps it; "make distclean" deletes it.

2)	Create the account the game will be owned by (and run as), if you
	don't have it already. As root:
		make install-user

3)	Run "make install" as root. This installs into /usr/local/lib/imperium
	with links to ConImp and TelImp in /usr/local/bin. You can change
	that and the owner on the command line, for example:
		make install IMP_DEST=/opt/imperium IMP_OWN=games IMP_GRP=games
	If you change IMP_DEST, the programs are rebuilt automatically,
	since they have the location of the game directory built in.

	Existing ic.allow and is.allow files are not overwritten.

4)	If you have trouble installing Imperium on your machine, let me know.

Running with systemd
====================

As root:

	make install-systemd

This does "make install" and also installs these services, which all run
as the impown user:

	imperium.service         the game server (ImpServ)
	imperium-telnet.service  TelImp, so players can "telnet host 3458"
	imperium-publish.timer   daily "ImpCtrl -s" to list your game in the
	                         public directory (not turned on by default)

Create a world first, as the game user (see Doc/impcre.doc):

	cd /usr/local/lib/imperium/data
	sudo -u impown ../impcre/default.sh

(Edit a copy of default.sh first to set the size, passwords, game name
and so on.) Then start the game and have it start at boot:

	systemctl enable --now imperium.service imperium-telnet.service

Useful commands:

	systemctl status imperium          is it running?
	journalctl -u imperium             server messages
	systemctl stop imperium            shut down cleanly (telnet too)
	systemctl enable --now imperium-publish.timer   list in the directory

The server also keeps its own log in data/imp.log. To stop the server
starting (say while you work on the files) create data/nostart. If the
server crashes, systemd restarts it after 10 seconds.

5)	Read the following files in the "Doc" subdirectory before creating any
	worlds for other people to play:
	hosting.doc, deity.doc, impcre.doc
